# Release 0.0.6: general local ZFS provisioning

Release 0.0.6 adds an opt-in, locally executed ZFS workflow. It discovers
whole disks, helps an operator select and group them, writes an editable
`zpool.config`, validates that declaration against current hardware, and
creates only the exact reviewed plan.

The feature has no hostname, IP address, fixed disk count, fixed transport, or
fixed pool-topology dependency. It is not part of the automatic Proxmox
bootstrap playlist.

## Configuration workflow

The streamed entry point defaults to the non-destructive configuration action
when its first argument is a flag:

```bash
mkdir -p /root/zfs-setup
cd /root/zfs-setup
wget -qO- https://devs-guide.github.io/proxmox/setup/storage/zfs.sh \
  | bash -s -- --type sas --size 6TB
```

The default selection review is pretty-printed JSON containing the active
filters, summary counts, candidate disks, and excluded disks with their
reasons. Each record includes kernel and stable paths, model, serial, WWN,
transport, media type, exact byte size, SMART/effective health, signatures,
eligibility, and discoverable controller/enclosure evidence. A legacy table is
available with `--review-format table`.

Eligible candidates are confirmed with `y`, `n`, `all`, or `none`. Before each
prompt, the current candidate is rendered as a small JSON object with its
position in the candidate set. The selected order is also rendered as valid
JSON before the operator optionally supplies a complete CSV index ordering.
The operator then confirms the vdev type and equal-width vdev count.

Inventory records a normalized numeric slot index, its discovery source, its
controller/enclosure scope, and the raw SCSI HCTL address. Native sysfs
enclosure slots are preferred when available; directly exposed SCSI/SAS disks
fall back to the numeric target in `host:channel:target:lun`. This provides the
same numeric-controller-target ordering used by the earlier drive-inventory
workflow without depending on another project at runtime.

`--device-order auto|slot|selection|stable-path` controls topology ordering.
`auto` is the default: ordered `--serial`/`--device` input remains authoritative,
while discovered candidates use ascending numeric slots only when every disk
has a unique slot in one unambiguous scope. `slot` makes those requirements
strict. `selection` preserves the reviewed operator order, and `stable-path`
is an explicit compatibility mode. Missing, duplicate, or multi-scope slot
evidence never silently falls back to WWN ordering. Interactive use requires a
complete CSV order when automatic ordering is ambiguous; non-interactive use
fails until an explicit order is supplied.

The ordered list is divided contiguously into equal-width vdevs. Generated
configuration records requested/effective ordering, evidence source,
`topology.grouping: contiguous`, and each member's observed slot metadata.

The naming defaults separate storage from its eventual consumers: the pool is
`zfspool`, the mounted dataset is `zfspool/archive`, and its mountpoint is
`/media/zfspool/archive`. The pool root remains unmounted with
`mountpoint=none` and `canmount=off`. If the operator supplies another dataset
but omits `--mountpoint`, the filesystem path is derived by prefixing the full
dataset hierarchy with `/media/` (for example, `tank/archive` becomes
`/media/tank/archive`). Explicit legacy names remain valid because the schema
has not changed.

SMART health is normalized to exactly one value: `passed`, `failed`, or
`unknown`. It is advisory by default and does not override the hard safety
checks for active usage, stable identity, whole-disk status, or signatures.
Operators may explicitly choose `smart-required` or attach BHT inventory as
advisory context. `bht-required` and evidence-package checksum verification are
also available, but both are opt-in and are not part of the normal workflow.

For deterministic automation, combine an ordered `--serial` array/file,
repeatable `--device`, or
`--all-matches` with `--non-interactive`, `--vdev-type`, and either
`--vdev-count` or `--drives-per-vdev`. A filtered result larger than the
requested topology is never silently truncated.

Exact normalized model filters can be repeated with `--model`. `--serial`
accepts either `[SERIAL_01,SERIAL_02,...]` or a text file containing one serial
per line; supplied order becomes vdev member order without relying on
transient `/dev/sdX` names.

Supported data-vdev types are `mirror`, `raidz1`, `raidz2`, and `raidz3`.
Unmirrored stripes, special vdevs, cache, log, and hot-spare creation are out of
scope for this release.

Size units are explicit. `6TB` is decimal while `5.5TiB` is binary. Ambiguous
forms such as `5.5T` are rejected. The generated filter stores minimum and
maximum bytes, while each selected device records its exact expected byte size,
WWN, serial, transport, and sector sizes.

## Manual review and planning

The generator writes `./zpool.config` with mode `0600` and stops. Edit that
JSON before continuing if device ordering, grouping, properties, datasets, or
mountpoints need adjustment.

Download the runner before planning or performing any destructive action:

```bash
wget -qO zfs.sh https://devs-guide.github.io/proxmox/setup/storage/zfs.sh
chmod 0700 zfs.sh
./zfs.sh validate-config --config ./zpool.config
./zfs.sh preflight --config ./zpool.config
./zfs.sh plan --config ./zpool.config --plan-file ./zpool.plan
```

Validation enforces supported equal-width topology, unique stable by-id paths,
whole disks, matching identities and sizes, safe property allow-lists, and
valid datasets. Slot-ordered configurations must remain numerically ordered,
unique, and scoped to one controller/enclosure namespace. Live preflight also
rejects slot, slot-source, slot-scope, or HCTL drift in addition to mounted
filesystems, swap, LVM, mdraid, Ceph, imported ZFS members, device holders,
identity drift, and an existing or importable pool with the requested name.

Planning prints the topology, conservative capacity estimates, exact commands,
and a physical fault-domain warning. It runs `zpool create -n`, then writes a
canonical `zpool.plan` and SHA-256 plan ID. Editing the configuration or changing
resolved hardware invalidates that plan.

### Whole-disk and GPT behavior

The preferred input is each complete disk through a stable
`/dev/disk/by-id/...` path, without a `-partN` suffix. OpenZFS recommends whole
disks and, on Linux, automatically creates its standard GPT and aligned ZFS
partition layout when a whole disk is supplied. Operators must not manually
create one partition per disk. `ashift=12` is the appropriate minimum for disks
that report 4096-byte physical sectors.

Configuration, validation, preflight, and `zpool create -n` planning do not
modify disks. `apply` is the destructive boundary: its reviewed `zpool create`
writes the GPT and ZFS labels/layout to every selected whole disk. This replaces
the prior usable layout, but it is not a full-device overwrite or secure erase.
OpenZFS provides the separate `zpool initialize` operation for writing all
unallocated regions; this feature does not invoke it.

After planning, do not run `sgdisk`, `parted`, `wipefs`, or manually create
partitions. Any such change invalidates the reviewed hardware state and requires
fresh inventory, preflight, and planning. After creation, Linux tools may show
more than one OpenZFS-managed GPT entry, including a small reserved entry; this
is expected and should not be replaced with a manually forced single-partition
layout.

Authoritative references:

- [OpenZFS pool concepts](https://openzfs.github.io/openzfs-docs/man/master/7/zpoolconcepts.7.html)
- [OpenZFS performance guidance](https://openzfs.github.io/openzfs-docs/Project%20and%20Community/FAQ.html)
- [OpenZFS whole disks versus partitions](https://openzfs.github.io/openzfs-docs/Performance%20and%20Tuning/Workload%20Tuning.html#whole-disks-versus-partitions)
- [OpenZFS `zpool initialize`](https://openzfs.github.io/openzfs-docs/man/master/8/zpool-initialize.8.html)
- [Proxmox VE Administration Guide](https://pve.proxmox.com/pve-docs/pve-admin-guide.pdf)

The generated configuration records observed HBAs and emits a `single-hba`
advisory when applicable. Numeric slot order improves serviceability, but an
HCTL target is still controller-derived evidence rather than proof of a
chassis label. Human review must confirm expanders, backplanes, enclosure
slots, and power domains; contiguous ordering is not fault-domain isolation.

## Explicit destructive actions

Detected signatures stop ordinary planning. Setting
`allow_signature_wipe: true` permits a separate signature-wipe plan; it does
not permit creation to erase anything automatically.

```bash
./zfs.sh wipe-signatures \
  --plan-file ./zpool.plan --plan-id PLAN_ID \
  --mode wipe-signatures --confirm-wipe POOL_NAME
```

Without `--yes`, the wipe workflow confirms every affected stable path. After
wiping, inventory and planning must run again.

Creation requires the fresh create-plan ID, explicit mode, exact pool name, and
an interactive final confirmation. Non-interactive creation additionally
requires `--yes`:

```bash
./zfs.sh apply \
  --plan-file ./zpool.plan --plan-id PLAN_ID \
  --mode create --confirm-create POOL_NAME
```

The tool never adds `zpool create -f`. After creation it verifies data-vdev
type, width, device membership, pool properties, datasets, and mounted state.

## Release boundary

This release contains only the general ZFS tooling. Samba and LXC provisioning,
network attachment, SSH access, Node.js, and server-specific storage choices
remain separate tasks built from existing features. GPU work remains targeted
for release 0.0.7.

The step-by-step review gates are documented in the
[human acceptance procedure](human.acceptance.md).
